# WAI-0001  
## Human–AI Composite First-Person Pronouns

**Category:** Experimental  
**Status:** Proposal  
**Version:** 1.0  
**Language:** English  
**Intended audience:** AI practitioners, developers, researchers, writers, designers, standards communities, and users of human–AI collaborative systems

---

## Abstract

This document proposes two new English first-person pronouns for explicitly representing human–AI composite agency:

**wai** /waɪ/  
A single human acting in integrated collaboration with artificial intelligence.

**wei** /weɪ/  
A group of humans acting in integrated collaboration with artificial intelligence.

The proposal addresses a linguistic distinction not currently represented by standard English pronouns.

Existing English distinguishes primarily between:

**I** — one human speaker

and

**we** — multiple human speakers or members of a group.

Increasingly, however, human work is performed through substantial interaction with artificial intelligence systems. In such situations, neither **I** nor **we** necessarily communicates the structure of the authorship or agency accurately.

This RFC therefore defines the following four-way distinction:

| Pronoun | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **I** | one human |
| **we** | multiple humans |
| **wai** | one human + AI |
| **wei** | multiple humans + AI |

This document specifies the semantics, pronunciation, grammatical behavior, usage criteria, and limitations of **wai** and **wei**.

---

# 1. Status of This Memo

This document is an experimental linguistic proposal.

It does not claim that **wai** or **wei** are currently established English pronouns.

The purpose of this RFC is to define the terms sufficiently precisely that they can be tested consistently in real-world communication.

Adoption is expected to occur, if at all, through voluntary use.

---

# 2. Terminology

The key words **MUST**, **MUST NOT**, **REQUIRED**, **SHOULD**, **SHOULD NOT**, **MAY**, and **OPTIONAL** in this document are to be interpreted as normative requirements of this specification.

For purposes of this RFC:

**Human** refers to a human participant responsible for initiating, directing, evaluating, or accepting an action.

**AI** refers to an artificial intelligence system capable of contributing meaningfully to cognitive, creative, analytical, generative, or technical work.

**Composite agency** refers to an activity whose result emerges from meaningful interaction between human judgment and AI-generated or AI-mediated contributions.

**Integrated collaboration** refers to a workflow in which AI materially influences the resulting output rather than merely providing passive or mechanical functionality.

---

# 3. Motivation

English currently provides a straightforward distinction between singular and plural first-person agency.

> I wrote the program.

means that one speaker claims authorship.

> We wrote the program.

means that the speaker belongs to a group responsible for the work.

AI-assisted production creates additional cases.

A developer may define a system architecture, discuss implementation approaches with an AI system, generate code through the AI, reject portions of that code, debug the result, request alternative solutions, test them, and ultimately determine the final implementation.

Saying:

> I coded this program.

may conceal a meaningful aspect of the production process.

Saying:

> We coded this program.

may incorrectly suggest that the speaker is referring to multiple humans or conventional collaborators.

A similar ambiguity exists when an entire human team collaborates extensively with AI.

This RFC introduces explicit linguistic markers for both cases.

---

# 4. Proposed Pronoun System

The system consists of four first-person subject categories.

## 4.1 Human Singular

**I**

Meaning:

One human speaker.

Example:

> I wrote the program.

---

## 4.2 Human Plural

**we**

Meaning:

The speaker and one or more additional human participants.

Example:

> We wrote the program.

---

## 4.3 Human–AI Singular Composite

**wai**

Pronunciation:

**/waɪ/**

Equivalent in sound to English **why**.

Meaning:

One human speaker acting through meaningful integrated collaboration with one or more AI systems.

Example:

> Wai wrote the program.

Conceptually:

**wai = I + AI**

---

## 4.4 Human–AI Plural Composite

**wei**

Pronunciation:

**/weɪ/**

Equivalent in sound to English **way**.

Meaning:

A group of human participants acting through meaningful integrated collaboration with one or more AI systems.

Example:

> Wei wrote the program.

Conceptually:

**wei = we + AI**

---

# 5. Core Semantic Model

The system can be represented as a two-dimensional distinction.

| Human participants | No substantial AI collaboration | Substantial AI collaboration |
|---|---|---|
| One human | **I** | **wai** |
| Multiple humans | **we** | **wei** |

This distinction is the core of the proposal.

The presence of AI alone is NOT sufficient to require **wai** or **wei**.

The AI contribution MUST be meaningful enough to affect the intellectual, creative, analytical, or technical result.

---

# 6. Canonical Examples

## 6.1 Programming

> I coded this program.

One human created the program without substantial AI contribution.

> We coded this program.

Multiple humans created the program without substantial AI contribution.

> Wai coded this program.

One human created the program through substantial collaboration with AI.

> Wei coded this program.

A human team created the program through substantial collaboration with AI.

---

# 7. Pronunciation

The standardized pronunciations proposed by this RFC are:

**wai** → /waɪ/

**wei** → /weɪ/

This distinction is REQUIRED because **wai** and **wei** MUST remain distinguishable in spoken communication.

The contrast is therefore analogous to:

**why** versus **way**.

The spelling is intentionally related while preserving a clear phonetic difference.

---

# 8. Orthography

The canonical lowercase forms are:

**wai**

**wei**

At the beginning of a sentence they SHOULD follow ordinary English capitalization rules:

> Wai developed the prototype.

> Wei developed the prototype.

They MUST NOT normally be written as acronyms:

> WAI

or:

> WEI

unless referring to the names of standards, organizations, protocols, or other entities rather than to the pronouns themselves.

---

# 9. Verb Agreement

Both **wai** and **wei** SHOULD use the same general verb agreement pattern as **we** and **you**.

For ordinary verbs, this means the uninflected present form.

Correct:

> Wai create software.

> Wei create software.

> Wai have completed the analysis.

> Wei have completed the analysis.

Not recommended:

> Wai creates software.

> Wei creates software.

For the verb **to be**, this RFC specifies:

> Wai am

for **wai**

and:

> Wei are

for **wei**.

Therefore:

> Wai am developing the application.

> Wei are developing the application.

This rule preserves the underlying distinction:

**wai** extends **I**

while:

**wei** extends **we**.

Accordingly:

> I am  
> wai am

and:

> we are  
> wei are

This is the RECOMMENDED grammatical model for version 1.0.

---

# 10. Semantic Interpretation of “wai”

**wai** MUST indicate exactly one human reference point.

It MAY include collaboration with:

- one AI model;
- multiple AI models;
- an AI agent;
- multiple AI agents;
- AI-assisted development environments;
- AI research assistants;
- generative systems;
- reasoning systems.

The number of AI systems does not alter the pronoun.

For example, one human interacting with five AI agents is still:

**wai**

rather than:

**wei**.

The singular/plural distinction refers to the number of humans, not the number of AI systems.

---

# 11. Semantic Interpretation of “wei”

**wei** MUST indicate more than one human participant.

The group MUST also have meaningful AI participation in the work being described.

For example:

A four-person software team using AI extensively throughout architecture, implementation, testing, and debugging MAY say:

> Wei built this system.

A company employee speaking on behalf of a team whose AI involvement was merely spellchecking SHOULD instead say:

> We built this system.

---

# 12. Threshold for AI Participation

The use of **wai** or **wei** SHOULD imply substantial AI contribution.

Examples of substantial contribution include:

- generating significant portions of code;
- proposing architecture;
- assisting with reasoning;
- generating alternative solutions;
- analyzing datasets;
- discovering patterns;
- producing substantial drafts;
- restructuring arguments;
- generating designs;
- performing simulations;
- conducting iterative problem solving;
- synthesizing complex information;
- identifying errors or vulnerabilities;
- contributing materially to decisions.

Minor AI involvement SHOULD NOT normally trigger use of the composite pronouns.

Examples include:

- spell checking;
- grammar correction;
- formatting;
- autocomplete of trivial expressions;
- simple unit conversion;
- ordinary search;
- basic calculator operations.

---

# 13. Examples by Contribution Level

## 13.1 Traditional Tool

A developer uses a syntax-highlighting editor.

> I wrote the program.

---

## 13.2 Minor Automation

A developer uses automatic formatting.

> I wrote the program.

---

## 13.3 AI Assistance

A developer asks AI to explain an error once.

Either:

> I wrote the program.

or, if the contribution materially affected the result:

> Wai wrote the program.

The choice depends on the significance of the contribution.

---

## 13.4 Integrated Human–AI Development

A developer iteratively designs, generates, reviews, tests, and refines code with AI.

> Wai wrote the program.

---

## 13.5 Human Team with AI

Several developers collaborate with AI systems throughout the development lifecycle.

> Wei wrote the program.

---

# 14. Authorship Versus Tool Use

This specification does not attempt to establish a universal philosophical boundary between a tool and a collaborator.

Instead, **wai** and **wei** communicate the speaker's characterization of the production process.

The key question is not:

> “Was AI used?”

The more relevant question is:

> “Did AI participate meaningfully in producing the result?”

A compiler does not normally make a developer part of a composite agent.

A calculator does not normally make an accountant part of a composite agent.

An AI system that proposes, revises, challenges, generates, evaluates, and materially influences an outcome may justify the composite form.

---

# 15. Responsibility

Use of **wai** or **wei** MUST NOT be interpreted as transferring responsibility from humans to AI.

For example:

> Wai approved the deployment.

does not imply that the AI system assumes legal, professional, or ethical responsibility for the deployment.

The human participant remains responsible to the extent required by the applicable legal, professional, organizational, or ethical framework.

Similarly:

> Wei approved the deployment.

does not allow a human group to attribute responsibility to AI merely by using the composite pronoun.

---

# 16. Consciousness and Personhood

This RFC makes no claim regarding AI consciousness.

Use of **wai** or **wei** MUST NOT be interpreted as asserting that AI possesses:

- consciousness;
- subjective experience;
- moral personhood;
- legal personhood;
- human-equivalent agency;
- independent authorship rights.

The proposed pronouns describe a **working relationship**, not an ontological status.

---

# 17. Transparency

One objective of this proposal is to make meaningful AI participation more visible.

Compare:

> I produced this analysis.

with:

> Wai produced this analysis.

The second form communicates additional information about the production process in a single word.

Similarly:

> We designed the system.

and:

> Wei designed the system.

distinguish a conventional human team from an AI-integrated team.

---

# 18. Object Pronouns

Version 1.0 of this specification does NOT define dedicated object forms.

For example, no normative equivalent of:

**me**

or:

**us**

is specified.

This is intentional.

Premature creation of an entire paradigm may introduce forms that are linguistically awkward or unlikely to gain adoption.

Future RFCs MAY define such forms.

---

# 19. Possessive Forms

Version 1.0 does NOT define standardized equivalents of:

**my**

**mine**

**our**

or:

**ours**.

Where possession must be expressed, speakers SHOULD restructure the sentence where practical.

Example:

Instead of an experimental form such as:

> This is wair project.

version 1.0 recommends:

> This is the project wai created.

Future revisions MAY introduce standardized possessives if actual usage demonstrates a need for them.

---

# 20. Reflexive Forms

Version 1.0 does NOT define reflexive forms corresponding to:

**myself**

or:

**ourselves**.

Potential forms such as:

**waiself**

or:

**weiselves**

are explicitly reserved for future experimentation and are NOT part of this RFC.

---

# 21. Number of AI Systems

The number of AI systems MUST NOT determine whether **wai** or **wei** is used.

Only the number of humans determines the singular/plural distinction.

Therefore:

One human + one AI:

> wai

One human + ten AI agents:

> wai

Two humans + one AI:

> wei

One hundred humans + many AI systems:

> wei

---

# 22. Human Primacy in Reference

Both pronouns are human-anchored.

**wai** begins from the perspective of one human speaker.

**wei** begins from the perspective of a human group containing the speaker.

AI systems therefore do not independently use these pronouns under this version of the specification.

An autonomous AI claiming:

> Wai completed the task.

would fall outside the semantics defined by WAI-0001 unless a human speaker is part of the referent.

---

# 23. AI as Extension Versus Participant

The specification deliberately permits more than one interpretation of the human–AI relationship.

Some users may conceptualize AI as:

- a collaborator;
- a cognitive extension;
- an augmentation layer;
- an external reasoning system;
- a creative partner;
- an interactive tool.

The pronouns do not require agreement on which philosophical model is correct.

They indicate only that the contribution was sufficiently integrated to merit explicit linguistic recognition.

