# 41. Reference Grammar

Under WAI-0001:

| Pronoun | Meaning | Be | Have | Example |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| I | one human | am | have | I am building it. |
| we | human group | are | have | We are building it. |
| wai | one human + AI | am | have | Wai am building it. |
| wei | human group + AI | are | have | Wei are building it. |

Past tense remains straightforward:

> I built it.

> We built it.

> Wai built it.

> Wei built it.

---

# 42. Minimal Implementation

A community wishing to experiment with this RFC need adopt only two rules:

**Rule 1**

Use:

> wai

when the intended meaning is:

> one human + substantial AI collaboration.

**Rule 2**

Use:

> wei

when the intended meaning is:

> multiple humans + substantial AI collaboration.

Everything else MAY initially remain standard English.

---

# 43. Canonical Statement

The complete system may be summarized as:

> **I = one human**

> **we = multiple humans**

> **wai = one human + AI**

> **wei = multiple humans + AI**

Or symbolically:

```text
I   → H
we  → H+
wai → H + AI
wei → H+ + AI
```

---

# 44. Canonical Examples

> **I coded this program.**

One human coded it.

> **We coded this program.**

A group of humans coded it.

> **Wai coded this program.**

One human coded it through substantial AI collaboration.

> **Wei coded this program.**

A group of humans coded it through substantial AI collaboration.

---

# 45. Conclusion

Artificial intelligence is increasingly participating in human cognitive and creative workflows.

Standard English first-person pronouns encode the distinction between individual and collective human agency, but they do not explicitly encode meaningful AI participation.

WAI-0001 proposes a minimal extension.

It introduces:

**wai** /waɪ/

for one human acting with AI,

and:

**wei** /weɪ/

for multiple humans acting with AI.

The resulting system is:

**I → individual human**

**we → human collective**

**wai → individual human + AI**

**wei → human collective + AI**

The goal is not to redefine humanity, personhood, or responsibility.

The goal is simply to make a new form of collaboration linguistically visible.

The canonical distinction is:

> **I built it.**

> **We built it.**

> **Wai built it.**

> **Wei built it.**

**End of WAI-0001**
